WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:07.020
Film Courage: Did you plan to be a filmmaker? Mark Landsman, Filmmaker [SCANDALOUS movie] I always
loved movies so I never thought one day

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:12.599
that I was going to make movies until that
happened in college. But I was always

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.580
obsessed with movies and my
brother and I would go like when I was a

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.810
kid and suburban Chicago like movies
were like 350 so I would go to movies

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.759
all the time we would sneak into the
r-rated movies often because you could

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:32.340
and we just saw everything under the Sun
and it was like a big deal so you know

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.770
this is the time that we would wait for
hours and hours and hours for Star Wars

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.970
to open or the Indiana Jones movies to
open and you know I would be in life

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.660
like two and a half three hours waiting
and the lines would wrap around the

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.900
theater and um so I didn't plan to be
but it was always a passion and an

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.970
obsession of mine since I was a kid when
did it become something you had to do

00:00:53.970 --> 00:01:00.210
I took an incredible class in college it
was called the American documentary

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:06.690
tradition the professor showed us pretty
much the entire Canon of classic

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:11.040
American documentary films we started
with Nanook of the north we moved into

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.810
pennybaker and the Mazal brothers and
Barbara Kopple Rob Epstein and Jeffrey

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:21.270
Friedman really all of the major
documentarians of the day we watched all

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.810
those films and then we would sit around
and we would talk about them often

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:31.560
really emotionally or you know some
you'd be crying there'd be you know a

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:39.000
lot of debate and I was really my eyes
really were open to what film could do

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.150
the power of film to generate those
kinds of reactions and people really

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:46.380
knocked me out so that's when I got the
idea that this is actually something

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.729
that people do this is a profession and
that's when I started to pursue it

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:55.950
really after that college class what do
you get from filmmaking that you don't

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:04.500
get from anything else I think I need to
be a filmmaker you have to have any sort

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.209
of an innate curiosity about humans you
have to want to know about people you

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:13.190
have to want to know
about all the different shades they're

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.780
inside of them the light the dark
everything in between I think you have

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.150
to really love people because it's not a
solitary pursuit it's a communal effort

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.910
it's collaborative so it's a combination
of being curious about people and also

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.510
really enjoying being around people I
mean unless you're making little stop

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.780
animation films in your garage which is
totally cool and you're by yourself

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:41.030
you're gonna be with people and so
filmmaking is this incredible

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:44.810
opportunity to learn about the world
through the eyes of other other humans

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:51.140
and also to create these incredible
little micro communities who are your

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.640
collaborators you know your your writers
your cinematographers your producers

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.450
your production designers your everybody
that you get to work with it's like this

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:05.000
each film is this opportunity to be a
little mini mini family it can also be a

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.290
mini disaster you know or a big disaster
depending on so it's just this

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:12.920
incredible opportunity to learn a lot
about yourself by being around other

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.610
people you know who are kind of on this
sort of common goal you're all on this

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.010
ship together
trying to get to this place in the

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:26.480
service of a story yeah I think it was
at Francis Ford Coppola and I use this

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.850
quote a lot but nothing man bites chaos
like passion and you know just so-so

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.269
yeah like you're saying it could be a
wonderful experience or there can be a

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:39.890
lot of things that yeah it can happen
because it's it's high-stakes pressure

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:43.459
yeah yeah to make your day if it's you
know I'm sure with documentaries it's

00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:46.610
different like I think it's not any
different with documentaries I mean I

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:51.560
think people like to think that there's
documentaries and there's narrative

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.750
cinema and that there that there are
completely different lanes of filmmaking

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:02.390
but there's so much that's the same it's
still story it's still character you

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:07.280
still have to engage people in the most
exciting situations they're both for a

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.019
big screen so you have to think about
how you're capturing the images and what

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:14.450
you're you know the only difference
really is that with documentary it's

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.209
unpredictable you don't know what people
are gonna say you don't know what

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:21.380
they're gonna do in scripted for the
most part you are controlling all that

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.100
so there's a thrill to it
but I think all the same things that

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.790
I've learned in narrative filmmaking I
apply to documentary filmmaking a lot of

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:34.230
my favorite filmmakers are filmmakers
that have their hands deep in

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:39.270
documentary as well
Scorsese Spike Lee Bennett Miller people

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:43.800
who go out and make films you know about
the world and real people and then go

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.700
off and make incredible narratives as
well and I think a lot of the tools that

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:53.520
you learn as a documentarian are very
similar if not the same that you that

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.910
you need as a scripted filmmaker it's
story I mean really everything is in

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.780
service of the story the only difference
is that in documentary its life it's

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.690
true it's real and you're in service of
sort of doing the best job to bring that

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:11.370
to light but you still at some some
point have to craft where it's going and

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:16.290
even if it's showing you you have to
sort of paint you know for the audience

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:21.090
perspective within that two-hour
absolutely I mean you're still using the

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.900
same tools you're still you're still
using editing music you're still

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.160
deciding about composition and lighting
and and you're still using incredible

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.620
cameras that we have at our disposal
right now graphics and animation all

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.970
kinds of stuff I think what's really
exciting time to be a documentary

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:43.170
filmmaker because those tool kits are
expanding you know people's ideas about

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:47.730
documentaries are like really really
expanding now so you're seeing films

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:52.020
that are that you wouldn't have seen
back in kind of earlier eras of

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.770
documentary documentary used to be more
of kind of like frederick wiseman like

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.770
you go in you show up in a big hospital
or an institution you turn on your

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:04.350
camera you let life unfold I mean now
they become a lot different a lot more

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:10.170
cinematic a lot more I'd say engaging in
some ways like Sherman's march yes from

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.680
years ago and and and maybe just see it
today it would seem slow I still love

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.820
that film yeah and I love his little
like sort of talking to the camera and

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:21.630
and almost like a precursor to some
reality TV where you have people doing

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:26.430
these confessionals but a much different
feeling from it yeah I don't think you

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.580
would have

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.460
a Morgan Spurlock if you hadn't had
Sherman's march that's true you know

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.850
right

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.890
you

